turns.text:
If the connection drops during write(), the ROLLBACK attempt will also fail. This masks the original error with a new connection error from ROLLBACK, so the caller gets the wrong exception and can't tell if the write happened.

Worse, if the connection drops after the query but during COMMIT, the write may have actually committed on the server even though ROLLBACK fails—the caller gets an error but the mutation is already done.

The fix: catch the ROLLBACK error separately so you either report the original error or at least acknowledge the uncertainty.

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
