If the connection drops mid-query, the catch block tries to ROLLBACK on a dead connection. That ROLLBACK fails, throwing a new error that masks the original one. The caller never knows whether the transaction partially committed or was rolled back—they just get an error. The server may still hold an open transaction.

The fix is to guard the ROLLBACK: only attempt it if the connection is still alive, or handle the ROLLBACK error separately so it doesn't hide the original problem.

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // Connection is dead; original error is what matters
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error (connection dropped) instead of throwing the ROLLBACK error, and the caller knows the transaction didn't complete cleanly.

