turns.text:
Read the write() function: if the pool connection drops during `client.query(text, params)`, the query throws. The catch block attempts `ROLLBACK` on the dead connection, which fails and throws a new error, replacing the original. The finally block releases the broken client back to the pool. The new ROLLBACK error propagates to the caller instead of the actual write error.

The database server automatically rolls back the transaction when the connection closes, so the data is safe, but the client sees the wrong error message. A more graceful approach would catch ROLLBACK failures and suppress them, letting the original write error surface:

```javascript
catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // connection is already broken; swallow the ROLLBACK error
  }
  throw err; // throw the original write error
}
```

That way the caller gets the actual problem (write failed, connection lost, etc.) not a ROLLBACK failure.

