turns.text:
Connection drops during the write query → that query throws an error → the catch block tries `client.query("ROLLBACK")`, but the connection is dead, so ROLLBACK also throws, overwriting the original error → the finally block runs and releases the (disconnected) client back to the pool → the ROLLBACK error is thrown to the caller.

The caller sees an error but doesn't know whether the write happened. On the server, the transaction stays open until it times out, locking rows and consuming a connection slot.

If the drop happens after COMMIT but before release, the write is already durable and the request succeeds normally — release still works on a dead connection (it just drops the handle).

The fix is to catch the ROLLBACK error separately so it doesn't hide what actually failed:

```javascript
} catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // connection is dead; transaction already aborted on server
  }
  throw err;
}
```

That way the original error (write failed) reaches the caller, not the rollback error.

