turns.text:
A connection drop mid-write creates uncertainty about transaction state. Here's the flow:

If the connection dies after BEGIN but during or after the write query, the actual UPDATE/INSERT may have executed on the server. When COMMIT is attempted, it fails (connection dead). The catch block then tries to ROLLBACK on the dead connection, which also fails—that second error overwrites the original one and gets thrown to the caller.

The caller receives an error, but it's the ROLLBACK error, not the COMMIT error. They don't know if the write succeeded (uncommitted on server), was rolled back, or left a hanging transaction. The finally block releases the dead client, but the server-side transaction remains open until timeout.

The fix: catch the ROLLBACK error separately so it doesn't mask the original failure, or rely on server-side timeouts to clean up. You could also wrap ROLLBACK in a try-catch that logs but doesn't rethrow.

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // Connection already dead; server will timeout the transaction
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error for the caller and avoids double-masking.

