If the connection drops mid-query, the client object still exists but is dead. The catch block runs and tries `client.query("ROLLBACK")` on that dead connection, which throws its own error. That error masks the original one, and the transaction state is uncertain (the database rolled it back automatically when the connection died, but the client doesn't know). The `finally` block releases the client back to the pool, which may be in an unusable state.

The fix: wrap the ROLLBACK in a try-catch so connection errors during rollback don't hide the original failure.

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // connection was already dead; rollback failed
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error, swallows a rollback failure (which is fine since the DB already rolled back), and still releases the client.

